positive_text:
Let $M = \{1, 2, \dots, 2013\}$ and consider a circle $\Gamma$. For each subset $\mathcal{A}$ of $M$ (including the empty set), define $S(\mathcal{A})$ to be the sum of the elements in $\mathcal{A}$, with $S(\emptyset) = 0$. Can we assign to each subset $\mathcal{A}$ a distinct point $A$ on the circle $\Gamma$ such that:
1. No two different subsets share the same point;
2. All the assigned points form the vertices of a regular polygon;
3. If $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_k$ are assigned points (for some $k > 2$) that form a regular $k$-gon, then the sum $S(\mathcal{A}_1) + S(\mathcal{A}_2) + \dots + S(\mathcal{A}_k)$ is divisible by 2014?

A positive integer $ n $ is called *super special* if it can be written in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} $, where $ x, y, u, v $ are positive integers. Show that:
(a) There are infinitely many such *super special* numbers;
(b) The number 2014 is not *super special*.

negatives.text:
A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 - 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $, with the additional constraint that $ x = u $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2014 is not super special.

A positive integer $ n $ is called \textit{super special} if it can be represented in the form $ n = \frac{x^3 + 2y^3}{u^3 + 2v^3} $ for some positive integers $ x, y, u, v $. Prove that:
(a) There are infinitely many super special positive integers;
(b) 2015 is not super special.

positive_text:
A graph is a complete graph $K_n$ with $n \geq 3$ vertices. A path is defined as a sequence of vertices where each consecutive pair is connected by an edge. A special kind of path is a Hamiltonian cycle that visits each vertex exactly once and returns to the starting vertex. Edges cannot be reused by different Hamiltonian cycles.
a) Prove that for any prime $n$, the maximum number of edge-disjoint Hamiltonian cycles in $K_n$ is $\frac{n-1}{2}$;
b) Determine the maximum number of edge-disjoint Hamiltonian cycles in $K_9$.

negatives.text:
Imagine a forest with $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$), each living in a different cave. There is exactly one unique path connecting any two caves. Before the King of the Forest election, some animals go on a campaign. Each campaigning animal visits all the other caves exactly once, only using the paths, never switching paths between caves, and returns to its own cave at the end. Also, no path is used by more than one campaigning animal.

a) Show that if $n$ is a prime number, then the most animals that can go on a campaign is $\frac{n}{2}$;

b) How many animals can go on a campaign when $n=9$?

Imagine a forest with $n$ animals ($n \geqslant 3$), each living in a different cave. There is exactly one unique path connecting any two caves. Before the King of the Forest election, some animals go on a campaign. Each campaigning animal visits all the other caves exactly once, only using the paths, never switching paths between caves, and returns to its own cave at the end. Also, no path is used by more than one campaigning animal.

a) Show that if $n$ is a prime number, then the most animals that can go on a campaign is $\frac{n-1}{3}$;

b) How many animals can go on a campaign when $n=9$?

positive_text:
A group of 25 countries forms a committee and agrees on these rules:

(1) the committee meets every day;

(2) each day, at least one country must attend;

(3) the set of countries attending each day must be different from the set of countries attending on any other day; and

(4) on the $n$'th day, for every earlier day $k < n$, the group attending must include at least one country that attended on the $k$'th day.

What is the maximum number of days the committee can meet under these rules?

negatives.text:
The 25 member states of the European Union set up a committee with the following rules:

(1) the committee should meet daily;

(2) at each meeting, at least one member state should be represented;

(3) at any two different meetings, a different set of member states should be represented; and

(4) at the $n$'th meeting, for every $k < n$, the set of states represented should include at least one state that was not represented at the $k$'th meeting.

For how many days can the committee have its meetings?

positive_text:
Suppose $a$, $b$, and $c$ are real, non-zero numbers with $b > 0$. Suppose the quadratic equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ has two different roots, and these roots are also solutions to the cubic equation $x^3 + bx^2 + ax + c = 0$. Show that:

a) $abc > 16$;

b) $abc \ge \frac{3125}{108}$.

Imagine a grid with $n \times n$ squares, and suppose each square has a bug sitting on it. At a certain time, all bugs move to one of the neighboring squares (a neighboring square is one that shares a side with the current square). Determine the maximum number of squares that can be completely empty after the move, for the cases where
a) $n = 8$;
\text{b)} $n = 9$.

negatives.text:
Imagine a chessboard with $n \times n$ squares. Each square has one bug sitting on it. At noon, every bug moves to a neighboring square (two squares are neighbors if they share a side). Determine the maximum number of bugs that can remain on the board if

a) $n = 8$;

b) $n = 9$.

positive_text:
Imagine um anel feito com $m$ polígonos regulares, cada um com $n$ lados. As regras para formar esse anel são:
1) Cada polígono no anel toca dois outros polígonos;
2) Dois polígonos que estão lado a lado compartilham exatamente um lado;
3) O contorno da área no meio do anel usa exatamente dois lados de cada polígono.
Na imagem, há um exemplo com $m=6$ e $n=9$. Quantos valores diferentes de $n$ permitem que esse tipo de anel seja construído?

negatives.text:
Considere um anel formado por $m$ polígonos regulares, cada um com $n$ lados. As regras para formar esse anel são:
i) cada polígono toca dois outros no anel;
ii) dois polígonos que estão lado a lado compartilham apenas um lado;
iii) a borda da área no centro do anel é formada por exatamente um lado de cada polígono.
Na imagem, temos um exemplo com $m=6$ e $n=9$. Quantos valores diferentes de $n$ são possíveis para formar esse anel?

positive_text:
Considere um triângulo $ABC$, cujos ângulos são todos agudos.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics{attached_image_1.png}
\end{center}

Explique como construir um triângulo $DBC$ que tenha a mesma área de $ABC$, com a condição de que o segmento $DB$ seja igual ao segmento $AB$, mas que o triângulo $DBC$ não seja exatamente igual (não seja congruente) ao triângulo $ABC$.

Consider the polynomial $f(x) = 4x^{4} + 6x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 2003x - 2003^{2}$. Show that:
a) the local maximum and minimum of the derivative $f'(x)$ occur at positive values;
b) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has precisely two real solutions and determine what they are.

negatives.text:
Let $M$, $N$, and $P$ lie on the sides $AB$, $BC$, and $CA$ of triangle $ABC$, respectively. Draw a line through $M$ parallel to $BC$, a line through $N$ parallel to $AC$, and a line through $P$ parallel to $AB$. These lines intersect at a point $T$. Show that:

a) if $\frac{AM}{MB} = \frac{BN}{NC} = \frac{CP}{PA}$, then $T$ is the incenter of triangle $ABC$;

b) the area of triangle $MNP$ is at most one-third the area of triangle $ABC$.

positive_text:
Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all positive real numbers $a$ for which there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the following conditions:

1) For all $x$ such that $2 \leq x < 3$, we have $f(x) = a x + (1 - a)$;

2) For all real numbers $x$, we have $f(f(x)) = 3 - 2x$.

negatives.text:
Let $\mathbb{R}$ be the set of real numbers. Find all $a>0$ such that there exists a function $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with the following two properties:

a) $f(x)=a x+1-a$ for any $x \in [2,3)$;

b) $f(f(x))=3+2 x$ for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all $k > 0$ such that there is a function $f : [0,1] \times [0,1] \to [0,1]$ satisfying the following conditions:

a) $f(f(x, y), z) = f(x, f(y, z))$;

b) $f(x, y) = f(y, x)$;

c) $f(x, 0) = x$;

d) $f(z x, z y) = z^{k} f(x, y)$,

for any $x, y, z \in [0,1]$.

positive_text:
Consider the quadratic function $f(x) = x^2 - a x + a^2 - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Determine all real values of $a$ such that:
a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ satisfying $|x_1^3 - x_2^3| \leq 4$;
b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ is true for every integer $x$.

negatives.text:
Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:

a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{2} - x_{2}^{2}| \leq 4$;

b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ holds for all integers $x$.

Let $f(x) = x^{2} - a x + a^{2} - 4$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all $a$, for which:

a) the equation $f(x) = 0$ has two real roots $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ such that $|x_{1}^{3} - x_{2}^{3}| \leq 4$;

b) the inequality $f(x) \geq 0$ holds for all real numbers $x$.

positive_text:
Suppose we define a sequence $a_1 > 0$ and $a_{n+1} = a_n + \frac{n}{a_n}$ for each $n \geq 1$. Show the following:
1) For all $n \geq 2$, $a_n \geq n$;
2) The sequence $\left\{\frac{a_n}{n}\right\}_{n \geq 1}$ converges, and determine its limit.

negatives.text:
Let $a_{1} > 0$ and $a_{n+1} = a_{n} + \frac{a_{n}}{n}$ for $n \geq 1$. Prove that:
a) $a_{n} \geq n$ for $n \geq 2$;
b) the sequence $\left\{\frac{a_{n}}{n}\right\}_{n \geq 1}$ converges and find its limit.

positive_text:
Each cell of an $n \times n$ grid contains one of the numbers $-1$, $0$, or $1$. Is it possible for the sums of the entries in each row and each column to all be different from one another (i.e., $2n$ distinct sums), when:
a) $n=4$;
b) $n=5$?

negatives.text:
In every cell of an $n \times n$ table one of the numbers $-1, 0$ and $1$ is written. Is it possible the sums of the numbers in every row and every column to be $2n$ mutually different numbers, if:

a) $n=3$;

b) $n=5$?

positive_text:
Consider a four-sided figure $ABCD$ with side lengths $AB = a$, $BC = b$, $CD = c$, $DA = d$, and diagonals $AC = e$, $BD = f$. Show that:
a) $a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + d^{2} \geq e^{2} + f^{2}$;
b) if $ABCD$ lies on a circle (i.e., it is cyclic), then $|a - c| \geq |e - f|$.

negatives.text:
Consider a four-sided figure $ABCD$ with side lengths $AB = a$, $BC = b$, $CD = c$, and $DA = d$, and diagonals $AC = e$ and $BD = f$. Show that:

a) $a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + d^{2} \leq e^{2} + f^{2}$;

b) if the four points lie on a circle (i.e., $ABCD$ is cyclic), then $|a - c| \geq |e - f$.

Consider a four-sided figure $ABCD$ with side lengths $AB = a$, $BC = b$, $CD = c$, and $DA = d$, and diagonals $AC = e$ and $BD = f$. Show that:

a) $a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2} + d^{2} \geq e^{2} + f^{2}$;

b) if the four points lie on a circle (i.e., $ABCD$ is cyclic), then $|a - c| \leq |e - f$.

\text{The sequence } \{a_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty} \text{ is defined by } a_{1}=0 \text{ and } a_{n+1}= a_{n}+4 n+3, n \geq 1.\text{\par a) Express } a_{n} \text{ as a function of } n.\text{\par b) Find the limit}
$$
\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\sqrt{a_{n}}+\sqrt{a_{4 n}}+\sqrt{a_{4^{2} n}}+\cdots+\sqrt{a_{4^{10} n}}}{\sqrt{a_{n}}+\sqrt{a_{3 n}}+\sqrt{a_{3^{2} n}}+\cdots+\sqrt{a_{3^{10} n}}}
$$

positive_text:
A sequence of numbers starts with $m$ and increases by $2$ each time. Another sequence starts with $n$ and doubles each time. The total of the first $n$ numbers in both sequences is the same.

a) Show that $m+n=2^{m}$;

b) Find the values of $m$ and $n$, if the third number in the second sequence is equal to the $23$-rd number in the first sequence.

negatives.text:
The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $2$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $3$.

a) Prove that $m+n=3^{m}$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

The sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic progression with first term $m$ and difference $3$ is equal to the sum of the first $n$ terms of a geometric progression with first term $n$ and ratio $2$.

a) Prove that $m+n=2^{m}$;

b) Find $m$ and $n$, if the third term of the geometric progression is equal to the $23$-rd term of the arithmetic progression.

positive_text:
Let $M$ and $N$ be points on side $AB$ of triangle $ABC$, with $M$ between $A$ and $N$. Draw a line through $M$ parallel to side $AC$, and let it intersect the circumcircle of triangle $MNC$ at a point $P$. Then draw a line through $M$ parallel to segment $NC$, and let it intersect the circumcircle of triangle $AMC$ at a point $Q$. Similarly, draw a line through $N$ parallel to side $BC$, and let it intersect the circumcircle of triangle $MNC$ at a point $K$. Then draw a line through $N$ parallel to segment $MC$, and let it intersect the circumcircle of triangle $BNC$ at a point $L$. Show that:

a) Points $P$, $Q$, and $C$ lie on the same straight line;

b) Points $P$, $Q$, $K$, and $L$ lie on a circle if and only if $AM = BN$.

Consider two sequences $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n=1}^{\infty}$ and $\left(b_{n}\right)_{n=1}^{\infty}$ where each term follows the rules:
$$a_{n+1} = 2b_n - a_n$$
$$b_{n+1} = 2a_n - b_n$$
for all $n$. Show that:

(a) $a_{n+1} = 2(a_1 + b_1) - 3a_n$;

(b) If all terms $a_n$ are positive, then $a_1 = b_1$.

negatives.text:
The sequences $\left(a_{n}\right)$ and $\left(b_{n}\right)$ are defined by $a_{n+1}=2 b_{n}-a_{n}$ and $b_{n+1}=2 a_{n}-b_{n}$ for every $n$. Prove that:

a) $a_{n+1}=2\left(a_{1}+b_{1}\right)-3 b_{n}$;

b) if $a_{n}>0$ for every $n$, then $a_{1}=b_{1}$.

positive_text:
Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram. Points $E$ and $F$ lie on the sides $AB$ and $BC$, respectively. It is given that line $DE$ splits angle $\angle ADF$ into two equal parts and that the sum of the lengths $AE$ and $CF$ is equal to $DF$. A line is drawn through point $C$ that is perpendicular to $DE$; this line intersects side $AD$ at point $L$ and diagonal $BD$ at point $H$. Let $N$ be the point where $DE$ meets $AC$. Prove the following:

a) The segment $AE$ is equal in length to segment $DL$;

b) If the line segment $HN$ is parallel to side $AD$, then side $BC$ is equal in length to side $CD$;

c) If $HN$ is parallel to $AD$, then the parallelogram $ABCD$ is a square.

A sequence $\{x_n\}$ is defined as follows: the first term is $x_1 = 2$, and for each $n \geq 1$, the next term is given by $x_{n+1} = 1 + a x_n$, where $a$ is a real number. Determine all values of $a$ such that the sequence is:

a) an arithmetic sequence;

b) a convergent sequence, and find its limit in that case.

negatives.text:
A sequence $\{x_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ starts with $x_{1} = 2$, and each next term is given by the rule $x_{n+1} = 1 + a x_{n}$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all values of $a$ such that the sequence is:

a) a geometric sequence (i.e., the ratio between terms is constant);

b) convergent and find its limit.

A sequence $\{x_{n}\}_{n=1}^{\infty}$ starts with $x_{1} = 2$, and each next term is given by the rule $x_{n+1} = 2 + a x_{n}$, where $a$ is a real number. Find all values of $a$ such that the sequence is:

a) an arithmetic progression;

b) convergent and find its limit.

positive_text:
A number is placed in each cell of a $9 \times 9$ grid. In any row or column, the sum of every group of $k$ numbers is also in that row or column. What is the smallest number of zeros that can appear in the grid if:

a) $k = 5$;

b) $k = 8$?

negatives.text:
Each cell of a $9 \times 9$ grid contains an integer. If you add any $k$ numbers from the same row or column, their total is also in that row or column. What is the minimum number of ones that must appear in the grid if:

a) $k = 5$;

b) $k = 8$?

positive_text:
Suppose we have a function $f$ that works with all positive whole numbers and follows these rules:
1) The function gives a whole number for any positive whole number input;
2) The function gives the value $f(2) = 2$;
3) For any two positive whole numbers $m$ and $n$, $f(m \cdot n) = f(m) \cdot f(n)$;
4) If $m > n$, then $f(m) > f(n)$.
Show that for any positive whole number $n$, $f(n) = n$.

negatives.text:
Let $f$ be a function with the following properties:
1) $f(n)$ is defined for every positive integer $n$;
2) $f(n)$ is an integer;
3) $f(2)=4$;
4) $f(mn)=f(m) f(n)$ for all $m$ and $n$;
5) $f(m) > f(n)$ whenever $m > n$.
Prove that $f(n)=n$.

positive_text:
\text{Solve the following problems:}

\text{i. Find a single inequality equivalent to both } x < \frac{1}{4x} \text{ and } x < 0.

\text{ii. What is the largest whole number that satisfies both } 4x + 13 < 0 \text{ and } x^2 + 3x > 16?

\text{iii. Find a rational number between } \frac{11}{24} \text{ and } \frac{6}{13}.

\text{iv. Write } 100000 \text{ as the product of two numbers, neither of which is a multiple of } 10.

\text{v. Calculate the value of the expression}
$$
\frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}
$$
\text{ without using a calculator or logarithm tables.}

negatives.text:
\text{i. Solve the simultaneous inequalities, } x > \frac{1}{4x} \text{ and } x < 0; \text{ i.e., find a single inequality equivalent to the two given simultaneous inequalities.}
\text{ii. What is the greatest integer which satisfies both inequalities } 4x + 13 < 0 \text{ and } x^2 + 3x < 16.
\text{iii. Give a rational number between } \frac{11}{24} \text{ and } \frac{6}{13}.
\text{iv. Express } 100000 \text{ as a product of two integers neither of which is an integral multiple of } 10.
\text{v. Without the use of logarithm tables evaluate } \frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}

\text{i. Solve the simultaneous inequalities, } x < \frac{1}{4x} \text{ and } x > 0; \text{ i.e., find a single inequality equivalent to the two given simultaneous inequalities.}
\text{ii. What is the greatest integer which satisfies both inequalities } 4x + 13 < 0 \text{ and } x^2 + 3x > 16.
\text{iii. Give a rational number between } \frac{11}{24} \text{ and } \frac{6}{13}.
\text{iv. Express } 100000 \text{ as a product of two integers neither of which is an integral multiple of } 10.
\text{v. Without the use of logarithm tables evaluate } \frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}

\text{i. Solve the simultaneous inequalities, } x < \frac{1}{4x} \text{ and } x < 0; \text{ i.e., find a single inequality equivalent to the two given simultaneous inequalities.}
\text{ii. What is the greatest integer which satisfies both inequalities } 4x + 13 < 0 \text{ and } x^2 - 3x > 16.
\text{iii. Give a rational number between } \frac{11}{24} \text{ and } \frac{6}{13}.
\text{iv. Express } 100000 \text{ as a product of two integers neither of which is an integral multiple of } 10.
\text{v. Without the use of logarithm tables evaluate } \frac{1}{\log_{2} 36} + \frac{1}{\log_{3} 36}

positive_text:
Consider a sequence $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, \ldots$ with $a_{1} = 1$. For any positive integer $k$, the terms of the sequence are defined as follows:
$$
\begin{gathered}
a_{2k} = 
\begin{cases}
2a_k & \text{if } k \text{ is even} \\
2a_k + 1 & \text{if } k \text{ is odd}
\end{cases} \\
a_{2k+1} = 
\begin{cases}
2a_k & \text{if } k \text{ is odd} \\
2a_k + 1 & \text{if } k \text{ is even}
\end{cases}
\end{gathered}
$$
Prove that the sequence $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, \ldots$ contains every positive integer exactly once.

negatives.text:
Let $ x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, \ldots $ be a sequence such that $ x_{1} = 1 $ and, for $ k > 0 $, is defined by the relationship:
$$
\begin{gathered}
x_{2k} = 
\begin{cases}
2 x_{k} & \text{if } k \text{ is even} \\
2 x_{k} + 1 & \text{if } k \text{ is odd}
\end{cases} \\
x_{2k+1} = 
\begin{cases}
2 x_{k} & \text{if } k \text{ is odd} \\
2 x_{k} + 1 & \text{if } k \text{ is even}
\end{cases}
\end{gathered}
$$
Show that the sequence $ x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, \ldots $ takes on every positive integer value more than once.

positive_text:
Consider triangle $\triangle ABC$. Points $D$ and $E$ lie on the sides $AB$ and $AC$, respectively, and point $F$ lies on the line segment $DE$. Suppose that $\frac{AD}{AB} = x$, $\frac{AE}{AC} = y$, and $\frac{DF}{DE} = z$. Show that

(1) The area of triangle $\triangle BDF$ is equal to $(1 - x) y z$ times the area of triangle $\triangle ABC$, and the area of triangle $\triangle CEF$ is equal to $x (1 - y) (1 - z)$ times the area of triangle $\triangle ABC$;

(2) The sum of the cube roots of the areas of triangles $\triangle BDF$ and $\triangle CEF$ is less than or equal to the cube root of the area of triangle $\triangle ABC$.

In triangle $\triangle PBC$, it is given that $\angle PBC = 60^\circ$. The tangent to the circumcircle $w$ of triangle $\triangle PBC$ at point $P$ meets line $CB$ at point $A$. Points $D$ and $E$ lie on segment $PA$ and circle $w$, respectively, such that $\angle DBE = 90^\circ$ and $PD = PE$. Let $F$ be the intersection of lines $BE$ and $PC$. It is also given that lines $AF$, $BP$, and $CD$ meet at a single point.
(1) Prove that $BF$ is the angle bisector of $\angle PBC$;
(2) Find the value of $\tan \angle PCB$.

In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$ ($\angle A < \angle B$), let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of the arcs $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$, respectively, on the circumcircle of $\triangle ABC$. Draw a line through point $C$ parallel to segment $MN$, and let it intersect the circumcircle again at point $P$. Let $I$ be the incenter of triangle $\triangle ABC$. Extend the line $PI$ to intersect the circumcircle again at point $T$.
(1) Show that $MP \times MT = NP \times NT$;
(2) Suppose $Q$ is a point on arc $\widehat{AB}$ (not containing $C$) such that $Q \ne A, T, B$. Let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the incenters of triangles $\triangle AQC$ and $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Show that the points $Q, I_1, I_2, T$ lie on the same circle.

negatives.text:
In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of arcs $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$, respectively, on the circumcircle of the triangle. Draw a line through $C$ perpendicular to $MN$, intersecting the circumcircle again at point $P$. Let $I$ be the incenter of $\triangle ABC$, and extend $PI$ to meet the circle again at point $T$.

(1) Show that the product $MP \times MT$ is equal to $NP \times NT$;

(2) For any point $Q$ (not equal to $A, T, B$) on arc $\widehat{AB}$ not containing $C$, let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the incenters of $\triangle AQC$ and $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Show that the points $Q$, $I_1$, $I_2$, and $T$ lie on the same circle.

In an acute triangle $\triangle ABC$, let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of arcs $\widehat{BC}$ and $\widehat{AC}$, respectively, on the circumcircle of the triangle. Draw a line through $C$ parallel to $MN$, intersecting the circumcircle again at point $P$. Let $I$ be the centroid of $\triangle ABC$, and extend $PI$ to meet the circle again at point $T$.

(1) Show that the product $MP \times MT$ is equal to $NP \times NT$;

(2) For any point $Q$ (not equal to $A, T, B$) on arc $\widehat{AB}$ not containing $C$, let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the incenters of $\triangle AQC$ and $\triangle QCB$, respectively. Show that the points $Q$, $I_1$, $I_2$, and $T$ lie on the same circle.

positive_text:
Let $m$ and $k$ be non-negative integers, and let $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ be a prime number. Show that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. The smallest positive integer $n$ for which $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$ is $n = 2^{m+1} p^k$.

negatives.text:
Suppose that $m$ and $k$ are non-negative integers, and $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is a prime number. Prove that

a. $2^{2^{m+1}} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^k}$;

b. $2^{m+1} p^k$ is the smallest positive integer $n$ satisfying the congruence equation $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^k}$.

Suppose that $m$ and $k$ are non-negative integers, and $p = 2^{2^m} + 1$ is a prime number. Prove that

a. $2^{2^m} p^k \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$;

b. $2^m p^k$ is the smallest positive integer $n$ satisfying the congruence equation $2^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^{k+1}}$.

\text{(1) Prove: on the complex plane, for each line passing through the origin except for the real axis, there is at most one point } z \text{ such that } \frac{1+z^{64}}{z^{23}} \text{ is a real number.}

\text{(2) Prove: for any complex number } a \ne 0 \text{ and any real number } \theta, \text{ the equation}
}
\begin{equation*}
1 + z^{23} + a z^{64} = 0
\end{equation*}
\text{has at least one root in the set}
}
\begin{equation*}
S_{\theta} = \{z \in \mathbb{C} \mid \mathrm{Re}(z \cdot e^{-i\theta}) \ge |z| \cdot \cos \frac{\pi}{10}\}.
\end{equation*}

